</div><p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0; text-align: justify">Healthcare reform laws, including the PPACA and
PAMA, are significantly affecting the U.S. healthcare and medical services industry. Recently passed legislation and possible future legal
and regulatory changes, including potential repeal or modification of the PPACA, or approval of health plans that allow lower levels of
coverage for preventive services, could substantially change the structure and finances of the health insurance system and the methodology
for reimbursing medical services, drugs and devices, including our current and future products and services. Healthcare reforms, which
may intend to reduce healthcare costs, may have the effect of discouraging third-party payors from covering certain kinds of medical products
and services, particularly newly developed technologies, such as our current products, or any other products or services we develop. We
cannot predict whether future healthcare reform initiatives will be implemented at the federal or state level or the effect any such future
legislation or regulation will have on us. The taxes imposed by new legislation, cost reduction measures and the expansion in the government&#8217;s
role in the U.S. healthcare industry may result in decreased profits to us, which may adversely affect our business, financial condition
and results of operations.</p><div>

